developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense. Let's get into it. So imagine

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:19.260
you have a really talented friend who knows exactly how to make your video games run super smooth

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.880
on any computer. That is basically what happened today. Researchers from right now AI just released

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:31.080
something called auto kernel. It is an open source framework that uses an autonomous AI agent

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:37.580
to automatically optimize GPU kernel code for any pie torch model. Here is what that means in

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:44.160
plain English writing fast code that runs efficiently on a graphics card. The GPU is normally one of

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:50.060
the hardest jobs in AI engineering. It requires deep expertise and lots of trial and error.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:56.780
Auto kernel lets an LLM agent, a smart AI helper that can think, try things and learn from

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:02.000
mistakes, handle this whole process in a loop. You give it a regular pie torch model, the kind

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.220
used in many image or language AIs, and the agent works to make the underlying GPU code run faster.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.140
You do not have to write any tricky low level instructions yourself. Think of it like having a

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.940
super smart friend who watches you play a video game. Then that friend rewrites the game's engine

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.880
on the fly to make it run smoother on your computer. Instead of you learning all the secret tricks,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.680
the AI agent experiments, measures speed, and keeps improving until the model performs better.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.640
This matters because right now the fastest AI models often need experts who can write specialized

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.360
GPU code. That slows down innovation and makes powerful AI more expensive to run.

