00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:37.480
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show. Let's do a deep dive into how AI vision models

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:42.680
sometimes break during multi-step reasoning. And how a new project called HopChain helps fix it.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:48.120
You know how when you are solving a puzzle in a video game, one tiny mistake early on can make

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.380
you lose the whole level. That is basically what happens with many AI vision models when they try

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:58.240
to reason about images in several steps. Imagine you and a friend are describing a photo of a birthday

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.140
party over text. Your friend says there is a cake. Then later the cake has candles. Then the candles

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:07.500
are lit so it must be someone's birthday. But if your friend misread the first photo and thought

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.820
the cake was actually a pizza, every later conclusion would be wrong. The mistakes compound.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:17.900
AI vision models do something similar. They look at an image, make a small observation,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:24.060
then use that to answer the next part of a question and the next. A tiny error in seeing one detail

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:29.760
like mistaking an object's color or counting items wrong gets magnified across multiple reasoning

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.780
steps. The final answer becomes completely off. Alibaba's twin team created HopChain to fix this.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:41.340
It works by automatically generating a series of smaller linked visual questions that force the

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.400
model to double check each detail before moving on. Kind of like a checklist that makes the AI

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.140
verify is the cake really a cake before it is allowed to decide whose birthday it is. And that is

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:57.640
basically how these vision reasoning problems work. Not so scary, right? All right, let's move on to

